  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nShowers and storms developed several hours ago across southwest\nNebraska and northwest KS and have been dissipating as they\nslowly track southeast. This activity has kicked out some\noutflow that is currently pushing through northwest KS.\nAdditional storms are currently moving through northeast KS into\nnw MO in an area of persistent 850mb moisture transport.\nConvection should continue to weaken early this morning with \nsome of this activity potentially affecting areas along and \nnorth of I-70 before sunrise. \n\nThe main question for today is whether convection and associated\ncloud debris will be enough to limit extreme temps. Current\nthinking is that there will be enough time for most of the area\nto recover by this afternoon. So went ahead and ran with another\nheat advisory for the entire area with the highest confidence in\nreaching criteria, along and south of Highway 56. With winds\ntoday not quite as veered out as Sunday, we may see slightly\nhigher dewpoints remain further west. Just like Sunday, Russell\nwill have the best chance to reach or break a max temp record\ntoday. This is mainly because the period of record for Russell\ndoesn't go back into the 1930s. Front will lift even further\nnorth for Tue, leaving extreme heat in place across the entire\nregion, with all sites seeing highs at or over 100 degrees.\nThere's a good chance we may need another heat advisory area\nwide for Tuesday. \n\nWe will start to see a slight pattern change starting Wed as \nsome shortwave energy starts to dig across the Pacific \nNorthwest. At the same time an additional piece of energy will \ntrack out of MB and into western Ontario. This will allow the \nlow level baroclinic zone to shift south and also allow some \nweak upper perturbations to lift out of the Desert Southwest, \nacross the Rockies and out into the Central Plains. While this \nwill result in high likelyhood of an MCS tracking across the \nPlains Wed night, confidence is high that it will unfortunately \nstay north of the forecast area. So confidence remains high that\nwe are in for more extreme heat for Wed afternoon and early \nevening. \n\nBroad southwesterly upper flow for Thu into the weekend will\ncontinue to allow a series of weak upper impulses to lift out \nof the Desert Southwest and out into the Plains, bringing \ncontinued MCS activity. The big question will be how far south \nthis activity makes it. Models right now agree on keeping most \nof this activity north and west of the forecast area for Fri \nand Sat, with increased chances for Sat night into Sun. So at \nthis time, it appears likely that extreme heat will stick \naround for Thursday and possibly Friday for southern portions of\nthe forecast area. Best case scenario would be that continued \nMCS activity north of us would push the cold front further \nsouth, bringing more relief from the heat for the weekend. \nHowever, confidence in this taking place isn't that high at this\ntime.",
